      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Antihypertensive agents, particularly diuretics and beta-blockers, can \"worsen\" certain coronary risk factors, e.g. plasma lipids, which could theoretically offset the benefit of lowering blood pressure. However, the use of such surrogate markers may be inappropriate; propranolol has been shown to significantly decrease the frequency of myocardial infarction (MI) as evidenced by the appearance of Q-waves on ECG, and diuretics have significantly reduced the frequency of clinical MI in the elderly patient with isolated systolic hypertension."},
